#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1de6657-82b1-5bcb-b8cc-fd2629d31faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10832" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' = \\dfrac {x^2}{y}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y^2 = \\dfrac{1}{3}x^3 + C.\n$$\n\nWe can express the solution in the form $y = y(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = 2C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068371 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0" :question/math-academy-id "e-10849" :question/problem "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10997" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84379651-35ea-52b3-97fa-614e5ead0f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{y^{3}}{3} + y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065717} {:answer/id #uuid "bddcf8bf-dea4-577e-b7b0-741fb768f4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sec (x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065718} {:answer/id #uuid "f184319f-a95e-5e73-a5b6-9827d78020db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\tan (x + C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065719} {:answer/id #uuid "2fe5eeee-833f-53b7-8b83-0a6fe948aa64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\tan (x + 1) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065720} {:answer/id #uuid "3463cd70-9638-5db9-9bdd-a3b32a75b6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\cot (x + C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f184319f-a95e-5e73-a5b6-9827d78020db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\tan (x + C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065719} :answer-field/id #uuid "07f31081-c281-5658-b7ef-4581c11a6aee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c17c4aa5-1ffc-5c4e-9267-85cce391b502" :question/math-academy-id "q-14257" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+1.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y)=x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a406919-2533-5bf3-87e6-cd3122ff57cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{y} = x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065813} {:answer/id #uuid "af89d087-1bce-5d10-b727-77195253adf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}e^{2y} = x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065814} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82afe8-e01f-578c-bbde-72a44be6e6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{y} = x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065815} {:answer/id #uuid "3212ed8a-aa8b-5826-9b94-3081f493c231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^{y} = x^{3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065816} {:answer/id #uuid "66d39395-7c92-5a4a-97ca-0e22e39188ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}e^{2y} = 2x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a406919-2533-5bf3-87e6-cd3122ff57cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{y} = x^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065813} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a47be1-a211-5316-bdf4-302ab41ba393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8653163f-c134-5fe5-8ca8-a073f08d08b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-154359" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}={x}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923d0c10-516e-522c-af35-b4848335d4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065849} {:answer/id #uuid "bcce1e70-26c0-5859-b057-fab6c48f781a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065850} {:answer/id #uuid "e115c657-7686-534b-8084-d76a998bb3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065851} {:answer/id #uuid "24dc42fe-bafe-5fea-98d3-b1147bb4f023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065852} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcc00ac-7620-515a-82e8-693fd76a876e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-x} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "923d0c10-516e-522c-af35-b4848335d4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065849} :answer-field/id #uuid "483dc0ad-506e-5219-8731-d4f7720ce3bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ed05f1b-222f-5d8f-b307-8248e81b3d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157692" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+y}\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n{e}^{-y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326" :knowledge-point/title "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "200ff40b-c483-5511-ada4-9962f1c23d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290078} {:answer/id #uuid "549aa129-3c38-5279-aaf0-8fc6677cb0c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290079} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6f447c-5fc8-5dfe-8140-683e78c870c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290080} {:answer/id #uuid "91acb97c-fd3b-5dfa-afb4-225be15bbf45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290081} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba46a08-ea30-56e0-baae-58f6e1ba67d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba46a08-ea30-56e0-baae-58f6e1ba67d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290082} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ebceda-4b19-5c0e-a39b-5accbe931e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb5bb3ab-6532-5577-9047-8f7af02e49c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-158026" :question/problem "Separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{g(y)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67b149c-f701-5be0-868d-c2f4a4be4be4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bae4517-ca75-59cb-a32f-b2a2a2c2eaa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290092} {:answer/id #uuid "0aeecf5d-5702-513e-801c-5c126e01c8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290093} {:answer/id #uuid "17ce1359-aa2a-5472-9c96-f2901f6cba5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290094} {:answer/id #uuid "222d92b3-2dcd-5344-b8b0-8d766efd687a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290095} {:answer/id #uuid "201dba49-412a-5f39-a491-3eaaf266d447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "201dba49-412a-5f39-a491-3eaaf266d447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290096} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0239e82-ea6b-51fb-b67b-3105cf1e47e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575d49f3-8bd3-5463-bab9-a94f8bc81fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158035" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & ={x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={x}^{3}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f0876a-33f0-5c6b-9195-8b9a0a550655" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ce0949a-0ea0-5360-adbf-f161a9ff3f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295232} {:answer/id #uuid "a387ccd1-f06b-5ffe-b783-cb8b81fbbe33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295233} {:answer/id #uuid "ac82fc84-dfaa-51f4-b138-ae94c4b491b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[3]{6x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295234} {:answer/id #uuid "d280d431-b9b1-558d-accc-d726f6290102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=({x}^{2}+C)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295235} {:answer/id #uuid "6642f64e-99b2-5e95-9f2a-31c65e5a51e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(6x+C)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d280d431-b9b1-558d-accc-d726f6290102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=({x}^{2}+C)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295235} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1296824-4d0d-5645-aae2-6b215316cc08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52368e24-e78c-5f40-b95b-9857942b42f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-51256" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6x\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6x\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\cdot 3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\int {y}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {y}^{1/3} & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/3}={x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}+C)^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e43912-62f7-5a33-9a76-7f51fd9f31ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x^{2} + x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068286} {:answer/id #uuid "b8596a92-ac9c-5b2a-850c-e7b27b6d6ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{x^{2} + x + C}{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068287} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1b0f02-f762-564c-aa7f-2380417c7edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{x^{2} + x}{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068288} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8e98ae-c8fc-584d-9fb4-b50ba813b1bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} + x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068289} {:answer/id #uuid "f67e1534-03a7-5e27-9835-eeecccbddce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x^{2} + x + C)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8596a92-ac9c-5b2a-850c-e7b27b6d6ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (\\frac{x^{2} + x + C}{2})^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068287} :answer-field/id #uuid "459788ea-078f-5030-8173-ec774f1d10ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "679701a5-b0c3-5865-adfd-e49e046da668" :question/math-academy-id "q-8303" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y}=2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873996aa-22f5-5b88-8e75-167e4b093cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ke^{\\sin x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068292} {:answer/id #uuid "9140f707-6730-50a1-804b-92b31cf5f648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ke^{-\\cos x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068293} {:answer/id #uuid "c96b87cd-4495-5236-a386-797ada3b613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ke^{\\cos x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068294} {:answer/id #uuid "e2eed065-175a-5a55-a12b-c3d1cc73ee5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{\\sin x} + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068295} {:answer/id #uuid "4904ed99-1755-5150-b2c2-74ae268ebcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-\\cos x} + K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873996aa-22f5-5b88-8e75-167e4b093cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ke^{\\sin x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068292} :answer-field/id #uuid "3886366a-b91e-5dfb-a240-a1c3975d5795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b946e806-cd3a-503b-8b7c-fe17b0906080" :question/math-academy-id "q-8305" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=0$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$"}]}
